<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fortress of Luxembourg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gibraltar of the North</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> was the fortified city of Luxembourg, strengthened over centuries by Burgundian, Spanish, French, Austrian and Prussian rulers until it was called the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> of the North'. After 1815 it was a federal fortress of the German Confederation held by a Prussian garrison. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1867">Treaty of London</a> of 1867 ordered its demolition; the remaining walls and the Bock casemates are now part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fortress-of-luxembourg.jpg" alt="Fortress of Luxembourg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown prior to 1867 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxembourg_fortress_before_demolition.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>In Roman times two roads, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arlon">Arlon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and towards Thionville, crossed on the plateau above the Alzette and Pétrusse rivers. Nearby, on the Bock promontory, stood a small fortification called Lucilinburhuc, a name that became Lützelburg and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. In its final form the fortress had three walls covering about 180 ha, when the city itself covered only 120 ha, with 15 forts in the centre, nine outside and a web of casemates cut into the rock. It controlled the Left Bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and the borderland between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Work on the walls begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">John the Blind</a> in 1320 went on to the end of the 14th century. In 1443 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> and his Burgundian troops took the city in a surprise attack by night, opening a long period of foreign rule. Around 1545 Italian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> engineers of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> built the first bastions. From 1672 the Spanish engineer Louvigny added towers in front of the glacis, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s siege of 1683-1684 Sébastien Le Prestre de Vauban rebuilt and extended the works with more than 3,000 men.</p><p>Austrian troops took the fortress in 1715, and under Charles VI and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> the engineer Simon De Bauffe gave it a triple line of defences. French Revolutionary troops took the city by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> in 1795. From 1815 about 4,000 Prussian soldiers lived among some 10,000 civilians. When King William III agreed in March 1867 to sell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for 5,000,000 guilder, Prussian objections set off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg-crisis">Luxembourg Crisis</a>, ended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1867">Second Treaty of London</a>; the Prussians left on 9 September 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The demolition was felt at the time as a liberation, and the city grew beyond its walls for the first time since the 14th century, along the Boulevard Royal and across the Adolphe Bridge. The Bock and Pétrusse casemates, the Vauban towers, the Wenceslas Wall and Fort Thüngen, now the Museum of the Fortress, remain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fortress of Luxembourg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the settlement of 1815 the fortress of Luxembourg, called the ‘<b>Gibraltar of the North</b>’, was a federal fortress of the German Confederation held by a Prussian garrison of several thousand men.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Military</a></li></ul></section>
